From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jrash <johnrash@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 10:20:09 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 304/342] net_socket: socket_acquire() doesn't bring a socket
 back from no references

It read the count, then added one, as two steps. The last reference goes
without the endpoint manager's lock that the lookup holds, so a receive thread
between the two could take the count from 0 to 1 while another thread destroyed
the socket, and its own release destroyed the socket a second time:
sSocketList.Remove() on links the first run had cleared, a fault at 0x168.

With a program closing connections with a reset while the peer keeps sending
(tools/probes/tcpchurn.c in the port's project), the panic came within about a
second, 5 times of 5, with memory to spare. With this, none.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/add-ons/kernel/network/stack/net_socket.cpp | 12 +++++++-----
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/add-ons/kernel/network/stack/net_socket.cpp b/src/add-ons/kernel/network/stack/net_socket.cpp
index b1716c0e85..e09e6008c5 100644
--- a/src/add-ons/kernel/network/stack/net_socket.cpp
+++ b/src/add-ons/kernel/network/stack/net_socket.cpp
@@ -609,11 +609,13 @@ socket_acquire(net_socket* _socket)
 	// endpoint protocol. We need to make sure the endpoint cannot acquire the
 	// socket anymore -- while not obvious, the endpoint protocol is responsible
 	// for the proper locking here.
-	if (socket->CountReferences() == 0)
-		return false;
-
-	socket->AcquireReference();
-	return true;
+	// The last reference can go at any moment, as that doesn't take the
+	// endpoint manager's lock, so the check and the increment must be one step:
+	// a count brought back from zero has the socket destroyed a second time.
+	BPrivate::WeakPointer* pointer = socket->GetWeakPointer();
+	const bool acquired = pointer->Get() != NULL;
+	pointer->ReleaseReference();
+	return acquired;
 }
 
 
